      "message": "It seems to me that this is always `nullptr` or did I miss the `alarm_new()` call somewhere?\n\n...anyway, shouldn\u0027t we also `alarm_free()` even when the alarm is not scheduled but `!\u003d nullptr`?\n\nUsually, if we want to only cancel the alarm it would look like this:\n\n```\nif (ase.autonomous_operation_timer_) {\n  if (alarm_is_scheduled(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_)) {\n    alarm_cancel(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n..but if we want to cancel when scheduled and also free the timer, then it might lo0k like this:\n\n```\nif (ase.autonomous_operation_timer_) {\n  if (alarm_is_scheduled(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_)) {\n    alarm_cancel(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_);\n  }\n  alarm_free(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_);\n  ase.autonomous_operation_timer_ \u003d nullptr;\n}\n```\n\n..but if we want to simplify, since `alarm_free()` cancels the scheduled timer, and is able to handle `nullptr` argument, it might be enough here to just do:\n\n```\n  alarm_free(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_);\n  if (ase.autonomous_operation_timer_)\n    ase.autonomous_operation_timer_ \u003d nullptr;\n```\n...or even this since this single assign is unexpensive even when it is already `nullptr`:\n```\n  alarm_free(ase.autonomous_operation_timer_);\n  ase.autonomous_operation_timer_ \u003d nullptr;\n```",
